## Why This Is Asked

Real-time audio paths require deterministic latency and predictable backpressure under concurrent production and consumption. This question probes lock-free design, ABA avoidance, memory ordering, and cache-awareness in a practical, high-frequency context.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free ring with per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA problems
- Deterministic latency guarantees for real-time audio frame processing
- Memory ordering: acquire/release semantics with proper memory fences
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing mitigation
- Backpressure without dynamic memory allocation

## Code Example

```c
// Ring buffer with per-slot sequence counters
#define SLOT_COUNT 256
#define FRAME_SIZE 64

typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t seq;
    char data[FRAME_SIZE];
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
    slot_t slots[SLOT_COUNT];
    volatile uint64_t head;  // consumer-controlled
    volatile uint64_t tail;  // producer-controlled
    char pad[PADSIZE];
} ring_t;

// Producer: enqueue frame
int enqueue(ring_t* rb, const char* frame) {
    uint64_t tail = __atomic_load_n(&rb->tail, Order::Relaxed);
    while (1) {
        uint64_t seq = __atomic_load_n(&rb->slots[tail % SLOT_COUNT].seq, Order::Acquire);
        intptr_t diff = (intptr_t)tail - (intptr_t)seq;
        if (diff < SLOT_COUNT - 1) {
            // slot available
            memcpy(rb->slots[tail % SLOT_COUNT].data, frame, FRAME_SIZE);
            __atomic_store_n(&rb->slots[tail % SLOT_COUNT].seq, tail + 1, Order::Release);
            if (__atomic_compare_exchange_n(&rb->tail, &tail, tail + 1, false, Order::Relaxed, Order::Relaxed)) {
                return 0; // success
            }
        } else {
            // backpressure: pause and retry
            cpu_pause();
        }
    }
}

// Consumer: dequeue frame
int dequeue(ring_t* rb, char* frame) {
    uint64_t head = __atomic_load_n(&rb->head, Order::Relaxed);
    while (1) {
        uint64_t seq = __atomic_load_n(&rb->slots[head % SLOT_COUNT].seq, Order::Acquire);
        if (seq > head) {
            memcpy(frame, rb->slots[head % SLOT_COUNT].data, FRAME_SIZE);
            __atomic_store_n(&rb->head, head + 1, Order::Release);
            return 0; // success
        } else if (seq == 0) {
            // buffer empty: block or return
            return -1;
        }
    }
}
```

## Microbenchmark Outline

1. Spawn one high-priority producer thread (audio ISR simulation) and one low-priority consumer thread
2. Generate synthetic audio frames at a fixed rate (e.g., 48kHz, 256 samples per buffer)
3. Measure enqueue and dequeue latency per frame using `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)`
4. Inject cross-traffic interference on a 2-socket server using `stress-ng` or custom load to stress memory controller
5. Record tail latencies (p99, p99.9) and jitter (max - min) over 10M+ iterations
6. Verify no lockups or data corruption under sustained 100% producer fill

## Expected Validation Criteria

- P99 latency < 1 μs under no-load, < 5 μs under cross-traffic
- Zero missed frames under backpressure conditions
- No ABA corruption across 1B+ enqueue/dequeue cycles
- Deterministic behavior across NUMA sockets

